<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Omdurman</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Omdurman is a city on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a>, part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>'s largest urban area. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> made it his base during the siege of Khartoum, and his successor the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallahi-ibn-muhammad">Khalifa Abdallahi</a> made it the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdist-state">Mahdist State</a>, where the Mahdi's tomb was built. On 2 September 1898 Kitchener's Anglo-Egyptian army destroyed the Mahdist forces at nearby Karari, in the Battle of Omdurman.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/omdurman.jpg" alt="Omdurman" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: عاصم الرشيد التوم · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Omdurman_Market.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Omdurman, in Arabic Umm Durmān, lies on the west bank of the River Nile, opposite and north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a>, in the State of Khartoum. The name means Mother of Durman, but who she was is unknown. It is an important road hub, and the Nile adds to its transport links. The climate is hot and arid, with only the summer months seeing noticeable rain, about 155 millimetres a year, and temperatures routinely above 40 °C in mid-summer. It is one of three connected cities, joined to Khartoum and Khartoum Bahri by several bridges over the Blue and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a> rivers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a>, and after the building of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>'s tomb following his death from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a>, the city grew rapidly. In September 1898 a British army of twenty thousand men under General Horatio Herbert Kitchener, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maxim-gun">Maxim guns</a> and Martini-Henry rifles, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>. At the Battle of Omdurman, fought in the nearby village of Kerreri, it faced more than 52,000 poorly armed Sudanese defenders, and the battle was over in five hours. At least 10,000 Sudanese were killed, against fewer than four hundred British casualties, forty-eight of them dead. Kitchener then ordered the desecration of the Mahdi's tomb, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a>'s words carried off the Mahdi's head in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerosene">kerosene</a> can as a trophy.</p><p>The following year British forces defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallahi-ibn-muhammad">Khalifa Abdallahi</a> ibn Muhammad at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-umm-diwaykarat">Battle of Umm Diwaykarat</a>, which secured British control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>. Kitchener restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a> as the capital, and from 1899 until 1956 Sudan was governed jointly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Most of Omdurman was destroyed in the battle, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>'s tomb was restored and refurbished.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Omdurman is the second most populous city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>. Its public universities include Karary University and Omdurman Islamic University, and its private ones include the Ahfad University for Women and Omdurman Ahlia University. Its location in the centre of the country, almost within the national capital, connects it by road to the cities and capitals of Sudan's other states.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Omdurman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nubian was spoken in central Sudan until the 19th century, Sudanese Arabic keeps many Nubian words, and rituals of Christian origin, chiefly the marking of crosses on people and objects, were still common in <b>Omdurman</b>, the Gezira and Kordofan in the 20th century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Ali Dinar, a grandson of Sultan Muhammad al-Fadl, returned to El Fasher after the Mahdist defeat at <b>Omdurman</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mahdi died in June 1885, and his successor, the Khalifa Abdallahi ibn Muhammad, ruled from <b>Omdurman</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/mahdist-state">Mahdist State</a></li></ul></section>
